course: biology
lesson: 138
title: Peppa Pig: what real pigs are like
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Peppa loves jumping in muddy puddles, and real pigs love mud too, but for a good reason: pigs can hardly sweat, so wet mud cools them as it dries, works as sunscreen and keeps off biting flies.
explain: A real pig walks on four legs, on the tips of two hoofed toes on each foot, and its flat snout is a strong, sensitive digging tool for roots and worms.
explain: Pigs are not dirty or stupid: given space, they keep their bed clean and use a toilet spot far away, and in one experiment pigs steered a dot on a screen with a joystick, a bit like George playing games with Daddy Pig.
explain: Peppa's family is all pink, but real pigs come in black, ginger, spotted and even curly-haired kinds, and they all come from the brown wild boar.
fact: Real pigs love mud: they have very few working sweat glands, so wet mud cools them as it dries, and the coat of mud works as sunscreen and keeps off biting insects.
fact: Pale pigs can get sunburnt like fair-skinned people, so farmers sometimes give them shade or sun cream.
fact: Pigs can be pink, black, ginger, spotted, belted or hairy, like the curly-coated Mangalica, while wild boar are brown or grey and their piglets have pale stripes that fade as they grow.
fact: Pigs walk on four legs, on the tips of two hoofed toes on each foot, with two smaller toes higher up that touch the ground only in soft mud and stop the pig sinking.
fact: Pigs make many sounds, grunts, squeals, alarm barks and rhythmic grunts when feeding piglets, and scientists could tell from recorded calls whether a pig felt good or bad.
fact: With space, pigs keep their sleeping place clean and choose a toilet spot far from it, so a dirty crowded pen shows a problem with the farm, not with the pig.
fact: In an experiment pigs learned to move a joystick with their snouts to hit a target on a screen for a treat, and pigs can learn their names and remember where food was hidden.
fact: A pig's snout ends in a flat disc with a tough edge for digging and many nerve endings for feeling and smelling; pigs were trained to sniff out truffles, though many hunters now use dogs because pigs eat them.
fact: Pigs are omnivores: in the wild they eat roots, nuts, acorns, fruit, worms and eggs, and their teeth have sharp parts for biting and flat parts for grinding.
fact: In many countries it is against the law to feed pigs kitchen leftovers, because meat scraps can spread serious pig diseases.
fact: The saying that a pig can never look up is not true; its short, thick, muscly neck just makes it awkward, and pigs see fewer colours than we do and rely more on smell.
fact: A mother pig, called a sow, usually has about 8 to 14 piglets; each piglet picks its own teat and goes back to it every feed, which cuts down fights at feeding time, and piglets double their birth weight in about a week.
fact: Farm pigs come from wild boar tamed thousands of years ago in Asia and the Middle East, and today wild boar even dig up parks at the edges of cities such as Berlin and Rome.
exercise: Why do real pigs roll in mud? | They can hardly sweat, and drying mud cools them | They like being dirty just for fun, like small children | Mud is their main food | Mud makes their skin pink
exercise: What else does a coat of mud do for a pig? | Works as sunscreen and keeps off biting insects | Makes it heavier, so the wind cannot blow it over on a stormy day | Hides it from farmers | Makes its bristles grow
exercise: What colours can real farm pigs be? | Pink, black, ginger, spotted, belted or hairy | Only pink | Only pink or white, as all farm pigs are | Only brown and bristly like the wild boar
exercise: What do wild boar piglets look like? | They have pale stripes along their backs | They are bright pink with big black spots all over | They are pure black | They are covered in curly wool
exercise: How does a real pig walk? | On four legs, on the tips of two hoofed toes per foot | On two back legs like a person, holding its front legs up | On four flat paws with claws | On its knees with its toes tucked in
exercise: When do a pig's two small back toes touch the ground? | Only in soft mud, where they stop it sinking | Every single time it walks, on any kind of ground | Only when it runs fast | Never, they are for scratching
exercise: Do pigs only say oink? | No, they grunt, squeal, bark in alarm and more | Yes, «oink» is the only sound a pig can ever make | No, they are silent animals that never make a sound | Yes, but they oink much louder when they are happy
exercise: What did scientists learn from recorded pig calls? | Whether a pig was feeling good or bad | What each pig's name was on the farm | Where the pig was born | How old the pig was
exercise: Are pigs messy animals? | No, with space they keep their beds clean and use a toilet spot | Yes, they always sleep in their own mess, however big the pen is | Yes, they never move from one spot | No, but only pink pigs are clean
exercise: What did pigs learn to do in a computer experiment? | Steer a dot on a screen with a joystick using their snouts | Type their own names on a big keyboard with their front hooves | Play football on a tablet | Turn the computer on by voice
exercise: What is a pig's snout used for? | Digging and feeling for roots, worms and grubs | Breathing under water when the pig is in a pond | Holding food up to its mouth, the way a hand does | Scaring away wolves and foxes by sniffing loudly
exercise: Why do many truffle hunters now use dogs instead of pigs? | Pigs like to eat the truffles they find | Pigs cannot smell truffles at all | Dogs are bigger than pigs | Truffles are scared of pigs
exercise: What do wild pigs eat? | Roots, nuts, acorns, fruit, worms and eggs | Only grass | Only meat | Only spaghetti, cake and other leftovers from people's plates
exercise: Why is it against the law in many countries to feed pigs kitchen leftovers? | Meat scraps can spread serious pig diseases | Pigs get far too fat on them and cannot walk | Leftovers are much too salty for a pig's stomach | Pigs refuse to eat anything that people have touched
exercise: Can a pig look up at the sky? | Yes, though its thick neck makes it awkward | No, never in its life, its eyes only look down | Only when it is lying flat on its back in the mud | Only baby pigs can, before their necks grow thick
exercise: About how many piglets does a sow usually have? | About 8 to 14 | Only about 1 or 2 | About 40 to 50 | Always exactly 3
exercise: Why does each piglet keep to its own teat? | It cuts down on fights at feeding time | Each teat gives a different flavour | The mother pushes them there | Piglets cannot see the others
exercise: Where do farm pigs come from? | Wild boar tamed thousands of years ago | Hippos tamed by farmers and kept in small muddy fields | Wild pink pigs of the jungle | Sheep that lost their wool
test: «Peppa's family is 4 pigs, if they were real how many legs touching the mud» | 16 | 8 | 4 | 12
test: «pig foot's got 4 toes but walks on 2, how many toes does a pig walk on altogether» | 8 | 16 | 4 | 2
test: «sow had 12 piglets, sorry, 11, each has own teat, how many teats busy» | 11 | 12 | 6 | 22
test: «pigs roll in mud coz they're filthy, everyone knows» What is right? | They can hardly sweat, so mud cools them and blocks sun and flies | Yes, pigs love being dirty for no reason at all, it is just what they like | Yes, they cannot find water | Mud is how they clean their teeth
test: In Winnie the Pooh, Piglet is tiny, pink and walks on two legs in a scarf. What is a real piglet like? | It walks on four legs on hoofed toes and soon doubles its weight | It walks on its two back legs from the day it is born, like a little person | It stays tiny for its whole life | It is born covered in wool
test: «my brother says a pig can never ever look at the sky» What is right? | Not true; pigs can look up, it is just awkward with their thick neck | True, their eyes only point down at the ground, so they cannot see the sky | True, their neck has no bones | True, unless they stand on two legs
test: «chuck the leftover ham sandwiches to the pigs, yeah?» What is the best answer? | No, in many countries it is illegal because meat scraps can spread pig diseases | Yes, pigs need meat every day, and ham and bacon are their favourite treats | Yes, ham is their favourite | Yes, if you take the bread off
test: «the pen stinks and the pigs are lying in muck, pigs are just gross» What is the real problem? | The pen is too crowded for them to keep bed and toilet apart | Pigs prefer sleeping in their own mess, as it keeps them warm at night | Pigs cannot smell bad smells | Pigs never use a toilet spot
test: What would happen to a pale pig left in a field with no shade or mud on a hot sunny day? | It could get sunburnt and overheat | Its skin would turn black to protect it | It would sweat a lot and stay nice and cool | Nothing, because pigs never get too hot
test: «pigs are thick, they just eat» What is right? | Pigs can learn their names, remember hidden food and even use a joystick | Pigs cannot learn anything at all, not even their own names or where food is | Pigs only know how to eat and sleep | Pigs are cleverer than any person
test: «wild boar in the park dug up the whole lawn, why» What were they doing? | Digging with their snouts for roots, worms and grubs | Building a nest under the grass to hide their piglets in | Looking for water pipes | Playing football with the turf
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the purpose of wallowing in mud by the domestic pig. | Pigs possess few functioning sweat glands, and mud cools the body while protecting it from sun and insects | Pigs derive their principal nourishment from mud | Pigs wallow solely for amusement without any benefit | Mud is employed by pigs for the cleaning of their teeth
exam: Identify the manner in which the domestic pig supports its weight in walking. | Upon four limbs, bearing its weight on the tips of two hoofed toes on each foot | Upon the two hind limbs in the manner of a human being, the forelimbs being held aloft | Upon four flat paws provided with claws | Upon the knees of the forelimbs and the hind feet
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cleanliness of pigs kept with adequate space? | They keep the sleeping place clean and select a separate place for excretion | They habitually sleep within their own excrement | They are incapable of distinguishing clean from soiled ground | They require the daily washing of their pen by the farmer
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the feeding of kitchen leftovers to pigs is prohibited by law in many countries. | Scraps of meat may transmit serious diseases of pigs | Leftovers cause excessive increase in the weight of pigs | Pigs refuse food previously handled by people | Leftovers contain insufficient salt for pigs
